# Review routing.
#
# `docs/05-delivery/52-governance.md` defines **area owners**: one per major crate, responsible for
# reviews in their area and for roadmap input, and not necessarily a member of
# the core team. This file is where that table becomes real — an area with no
# owner is an area where a pull request waits for whoever notices.
#
# ⚠️ **Today every line names one person, which is exactly the failure mode
# `docs/05-delivery/52-governance.md` § The bus factor rule exists to prevent.** It is recorded here
# rather than hidden: Moso does not make a public 0.1 announcement with fewer
# than three maintainers holding commit rights, and this file is one of the
# artefacts that must change before that announcement. When a second and third
# maintainer join, split these lines by area rather than adding everybody to
# every line — a review request that goes to all maintainers goes to nobody.
#
# Syntax note: the *last* matching pattern wins, so the specific entries below
# override the catch-all at the top.

*                               @lowsbarrel

# ── Design documents ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The documents are the source of truth (51-work-packages.md). A change here is
# a design change and is reviewed as one, even when it looks like prose.
/docs/                          @lowsbarrel
/docs/adr/                      @lowsbarrel

# ── The HTTP half ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/crates/moso/                   @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-core/              @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-schema/            @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-openapi/           @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-macros/            @lowsbarrel

# ── The data layer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADR-0005 (the sealed SQL facade) is enforced by `xtask check-sealed`, but the
# reason a foreign type would leak is almost always a signature that looked
# harmless in review, so these need an owner who knows the rule.
/crates/moso-sql/               @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-orm/               @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-orm-macros/        @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-migrate/           @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-kv/                @lowsbarrel

# ── The batteries ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Security defaults live here. `docs/05-delivery/52-governance.md` § The RFC process requires an RFC
# for any change to a security default, so a pull request touching these that has
# no linked RFC should be asked for one before it is read line by line.
/crates/moso-auth/              @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-authz/             @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-jobs/              @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-mail/              @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-storage/           @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-admin/             @lowsbarrel

# ── Tooling, tests and the diagnostics corpus ───────────────────────────────
/crates/moso-cli/               @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-test/              @lowsbarrel
/crates/moso-ui-tests/          @lowsbarrel
/xtask/                         @lowsbarrel
/scripts/                       @lowsbarrel
/compose.test.yaml              @lowsbarrel

# ── The gates themselves ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything that can *weaken a gate* is owned tightly: a workflow edit, the
# licence and advisory policy, the toolchain, or the lint configuration in the
# workspace manifest. This is the highest-leverage diff in the repository and the
# easiest one to wave through.
/.github/                       @lowsbarrel
/deny.toml                      @lowsbarrel
/rust-toolchain.toml            @lowsbarrel
/.cargo/                        @lowsbarrel
/Cargo.toml                     @lowsbarrel
/Cargo.lock                     @lowsbarrel

# ── Legal ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/LICENSE                        @lowsbarrel
